## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates your ability to design pluggable runtime extensions, handle concurrency considerations, and understand trade-offs between in-process versus out-of-process sinks.

## Key Concepts

- Plugin registry and dynamic loading at runtime
- Minimal interface contracts for telemetry sinks
- Backpressure, batching, and failure isolation
- Stateless versus stateful sinks and lifecycle management

## Code Example

```javascript
interface TelemetrySink {
  send(event: TelemetryEvent): Promise<void>;
  flush?(): Promise<void>;
  shutdown?(): Promise<void>;
}

class PluginRegistry {
  #sinks = new Map();

  register(name, factory) {
    this.#sinks.set(name, factory(this));
  }

  get(name) {
    return this.#sinks.get(name);
  }

  list() {
    return Array.from(this.#sinks.keys());
  }
}

class SyslogSink {
  #buffer = [];
  #batchSize = 100;
  #flushInterval = 5000;
  #paused = false;
  #controller = new AbortController();

  constructor(options = {}) {
    this.#batchSize = options.batchSize ?? 100;
    this.#flushInterval = options.flushInterval ?? 5000;
    this.#startFlushTimer();
  }

  async send(event) {
    if (this.#paused) {
      throw new Error('Backpressure: sink is paused');
    }

    this.#buffer.push(event);

    if (this.#buffer.length >= this.#batchSize) {
      await this.flush();
    }

    if (this.#buffer.length > this.#batchSize * 0.8) {
      this.#paused = true;
    }
  }

  async flush() {
    if (this.#buffer.length === 0) return;

    const batch = this.#buffer.splice(0, this.#batchSize);
    try {
      await this.#sendToSyslog(batch);
      this.#paused = false;
    } catch (err) {
      this.#buffer.unshift(...batch);
      throw err;
    }
  }

  async shutdown() {
    this.#controller.abort();
    await this.flush();
  }

  #startFlushTimer() {
    setInterval(() => this.flush(), this.#flushInterval);
  }

  async #sendToSyslog(batch) {
    // Send batch to syslog endpoint
  }
}

// Usage
const registry = new PluginRegistry();
registry.register('syslog', () => new SyslogSink({ batchSize: 50 }));
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of how to decouple variability in behavior using design patterns. It shows how to extend functionality without touching call sites, a common real-world need.

## Key Concepts
- Strategy pattern for interchangeable retry logic
- Dependency injection to supply policies
- Clear separation of fetch logic from retry strategy
- Testability by swapping mock policies

## Code Example
```javascript
interface RetryPolicy {
  shouldRetry(attempt: number, err: any): boolean;
  nextDelay(attempt: number, err?: any): number;
}
class LinearPolicy implements RetryPolicy {
  constructor(private delay: number, private max: number) {}
  shouldRetry(attempt: number) { return attempt < this.max; }
  nextDelay(_attempt: number) { return this.delay; }
}
class ExponentialPolicy implements RetryPolicy {
  constructor(private base: number, private factor: number, private cap: number) {}
  shouldRetry(attempt: number) { return attempt * this.factor < this.cap; }
  nextDelay(attempt: number) { return Math.min(this.base * Math.pow(this.factor, attempt - 1), this.cap); }
}
```

```javascript
const policy = new ExponentialPolicy(100, 2, 1000);
fetchUser('alice', policy);
```

